# Calibration for the clock the three workloads use.
#
# libc clock() returns clock_t; CLOCKS_PER_SEC is a property of the C runtime,
# not of the language, and the workloads report their timings as milliseconds on
# the assumption that it is 1000 on this toolchain. This program checks that
# assumption against an external stopwatch instead of trusting it.
#
#     dynlex clock_calibration.dl -O2 -o build/clock_calibration.out
#     time ./build/clock_calibration.out
#
# It prints the sum (7000000000, a fixed value, so the loop cannot be folded
# away without being wrong) and the tick count for the loop. Divide the tick
# count by the wall time of the process minus the empty-program baseline to get
# the tick rate.
#
# harness.py runs this program five times at each optimisation level and writes
# the whole table into results/dynlex-performance.md. Readings converge on 1000
# from below as the runs settle, so CLOCKS_PER_SEC is 1000 and a tick is one
# millisecond. A low reading is the external stopwatch running longer than the
# tick count, which is what a descheduled or cold-started process looks like.
#
# At -O2 LLVM strength-reduces this loop, so the same source takes about 0.23 s
# instead of about 4 s. The tick rate is what the program is for, not the loop
# time.

import common.dl

set total to 0 as a 64 bit integer
set started to the clock milliseconds
set index to 0 as a 64 bit integer
loop while index < 2000000000:
    set total to total + (index & 7)
    set index to index + 1
set finished to the clock milliseconds

print integer total
print integer (finished - started)
